;;; Sequence.mon --- Evaluation-parametric sequential observation
:bootstrap primitive

:author   Laluxx
:version  6.0.0
:keywords sequence coalgebra evaluation strict lazy productive lowering fusion state

;;; Commentary:

 Sequence describes one-dimensional ordered observation. It says what the next
 visible element is and how the continuation is evaluated; it does not require a
 particular container representation, builder, index operation, or evaluation
 policy.

 The architecture is factored along independent axes:

                    sequence shape × evaluation strategy.

 A strict sequence satisfies Strategy S ~ Strict. A lazy sequence satisfies
 Strategy S ~ Lazy. Neither proposition introduces another sequence class.

;;; Coalgebraic Semantics

 Fix a state type S, visible element type A, and Evaluation E. The sequence
 observation functor is

                    F_E,A(X) = 1 + A × E X.

 A Sequence instance is precisely an F_E,A-coalgebra

                    uncons : S -> F_E,A(S),

 represented in Core as

                    uncons :: S -> (A, E S)?.

 =nil= is termination. A present pair contains one visible element and the next
 state under the associated evaluation strategy.

 This is a class over the state type S itself, not over a required type
 constructor =C :: Type -> Type=. That choice is deliberate. Coalgebras are
 state systems, and useful sequential states may be monomorphic: a UTF-8 decoder,
 an integer range, a compressed cursor, or a parser token source can have one
 natural element type without pretending to be a polymorphic container.

 Polymorphic containers remain free to implement Functor separately. For
 example =[a]= is both a Sequence state and part of the Functor family =Coll=.
 Sequence-hood and representation-preserving mapping are orthogonal facts.

;;; Evaluation as an Associated Modality

 Every Sequence state names exactly one Strategy, and sequence-hood carries the
 corresponding Evaluation dictionary as a superclass invariant:

                    Evaluation (Strategy S).

 Therefore generic sequence code receives defer/demand and Applicative structure
 once from the instance. It must never rediscover or duplicate strict/lazy
 behavior in separate APIs.

 For Strict, the continuation is immediate. For Lazy, it is call-by-need. The
 visible coalgebra is otherwise identical.

 A future structure such as Tree should make the same separation at its recursive
 positions rather than inventing StrictTree and LazyTree semantics independently.

;;; Productivity and Finiteness

 Sequence alone permits divergence while obtaining a demanded observation.
 ProductiveSequence is an erased semantic certificate that the existing =uncons=
 coalgebra is productive: every finite visible demand performs only finitely many
 terminating Strategy demands and terminating observations.

 The class deliberately does not define a second observer. Doing so would repeat
 sequence semantics merely to attach a proof. Instead, the compiler validates a
 ProductiveSequence instance as a theorem about =uncons= and uses its dictionary
 only as static evidence for discharging the corresponding =diverge= effects.

 Concrete representations may make that theorem constructive. Control.Evaluation
 provides =TotalDemand E A=, an opaque refinement proving one demand terminates.
 Data.Sequence.Unfold uses such continuations to define sequence types whose
 ProductiveSequence instances follow directly from their representation. Other
 representations may be proved productive by guarded recursion, sized reasoning,
 or compiler-derived evidence without changing the Sequence interface.

 FiniteSequence strengthens productivity with well-foundedness: every continuation
 chain reaches nil after finitely many visible observations. InfiniteSequence
 instead proves nil unreachable while every finite visible demand remains
 productive. These are proof-only refinements, not runtime size computations and
 not inventories of collection methods.

 In particular, ProductiveSequence does not make unbounded search total. A
 productive infinite sequence may contain no element satisfying a predicate, so
 a search can make infinitely many individually productive observations.

 Certificate instances are not trusted assertions. The compiler must check their
 proof obligations before accepting an instance and may erase the resulting
 dictionaries after elaboration. Initially those proofs may come from structural
 recursion, TotalDemand-bearing representations, and a small set of compositional
 rules. Later guarded recursion, clocks, sizes, or user proofs may extend the proof
 language without changing Sequence itself.

 This policy is analogous to law-bearing algebraic classes such as Ring: Core
 names the semantic proposition; the compiler/proof layer justifies membership.
 Runtime observation remains owned by one =uncons= definition.

;;; Functor Is Orthogonal

 The old Sequence class inherited Functor because lists happen to be polymorphic
 containers. That conflated two different structures.

 A Sequence state need not admit representation-preserving mapping at all. When
 a type constructor is a lawful Functor, its own Functor instance owns that fact.
 =Spine E= and =Producer E= are examples: both have Functor instances, but
 Sequence itself does not require them.

 This separation is especially important for generic filtering. A filtered view
 stores a predicate over the source element type; pretending that such a view is
 automatically the same unary type constructor after mapping can be ill-kinded or
 semantically false. Producer existentially hides private machine state from its public yielded
 element type and therefore provides the honest representation-changing layer.

;;; Materialized Collection Compatibility

 The historical Core Sequence module also owns a finite same-representation
 collection vocabulary: length, reverse, indexing, take/drop, filtering, zip,
 and related list/String behavior.

 During this research refactor that API is isolated under MaterializedSequence.
 MaterializedSequence remains higher-kinded because operations such as zip and
 same-representation filtering are properties of a polymorphic collection family,
 not of the minimal state coalgebra above.

 Its current superclass is Foldable only. Normatively, every =c a= admitted by a
 MaterializedSequence instance is finite; a future quantified-constraint surface
 may state =forall a. FiniteSequence (c a)= directly. Until then this compatibility
 capability must not be mistaken for the mathematical definition of Sequence.

;;; Strict and Lazy Sequences

 =Strict sequence= and =Lazy sequence= are propositions, not classes:

                    StrictSequence S := Sequence S, Strategy S ~ Strict
                    LazySequence   S := Sequence S, Strategy S ~ Lazy.

 A future constraint-alias feature may provide those spellings without allocating
 dictionaries or attaching methods. There must remain exactly one sequence
 coalgebra and exactly one owner of each evaluation strategy.

;;; Producer Lowering and Fusion Boundary

 Data.Sequence.Producer owns the intensional transition functor

                    P_E,A(S) = 1 + E S + A × E S

 and existentially packages the private state S. Its middle summand is Skip, a
 silent machine transition useful for fusion. Skip does not belong to Sequence:
 it describes computation of the next visible observation, not sequence shape.

 =lower= is the sole bridge from extensional Sequence semantics to this machine.
 It has a default definition derived entirely from =uncons=, so an ordinary user
 Sequence still has one minimal semantic definition. A representation that is
 already producer-backed may override =lower= to return that existing machine
 instead of normalizing through a visible observation boundary.

 The coherence law is extensional and demand-sensitive:

                  uncons (lower source)  ≃d  uncons source.

 Here ≃d preserves the visible result, demanded divergence/termination, Strategy
 behavior, and QTT usage. Lowering itself is construction-total and demand-neutral:
 it may package closures and strict metadata but may not call =uncons=, demand a
 Strategy continuation, or diverge merely to construct the Producer. A specialized
 lowering may expose a different hidden Skip/state structure, but observing that
 machine may not demand more than observing the default lowering.

 This is not a second sequence observer. =uncons= alone defines sequence meaning;
 =lower= is a derived optimization witness with a default implementation. Its
 purpose is compositionality: nested views lower directly to one existential
 producer pipeline rather than repeatedly reifying visible Sequence boundaries.

 Producer is the Core-level reference calculus, not a promise that its existential
 packages are the compiler's final IR. Flat-map and stateful transformations may
 place nested producer values in hidden machine state. When the pipeline is
 statically known after specialization, the compiler must normalize that calculus
 to first-order control flow and erase composition-only representation. Keeping
 that richer normal form internal avoids adding a second public stream hierarchy
 merely to encode one optimizer implementation.

;;; Indexed Boundary

 The coalgebra above is regular: the continuation state has the same type S.
 Length-indexed vectors expose a genuinely different structure:

                    Vec (S n) A -> Vec n A.

 Likewise NonEmpty A exposes an ordinary list as its tail. These are indexed or
 family-changing observations rather than counterexamples to the regular
 Sequence interface. They should motivate an indexed-container/SequenceFamily
 abstraction only if several concrete users justify that additional theory.

 We deliberately do not hide the distinction behind existential packaging just
 to make the base class appear universal.

;;; Research Basis

 J.J.M.M. Rutten, "Universal Coalgebra: a Theory of Systems", Theoretical
 Computer Science 249(1), 2000.
   https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(00)00056-6

 Michael Abbott, Thorsten Altenkirch, and Neil Ghani, "Containers: Constructing
 Strictly Positive Types", Theoretical Computer Science 342(1), 2005.
   https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2005.06.002

 Duncan Coutts, Roman Leshchinskiy, and Don Stewart, "Stream Fusion: From Lists
 to Streams to Nothing at All", ICFP 2007.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/1291151.1291199

 Ralf Hinze, Thomas Harper, and Daniel W. H. James, "Theory and Practice of
 Fusion", IFL 2010.
   https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/daniel.james/fusion.html

 Oleg Kiselyov, Tomoaki Kobayashi, and Nick Palladinos, "Complete Fusion for
 Stateful Streams: Equational Theory of Stateful Streams and Fusion as
 Normalization-by-Evaluation", arXiv:2412.15768, revised 2026.
   https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15768

 Rutten supplies the state-coalgebra viewpoint. Containers explain the separate
 functorial structure of polymorphic datatype families. Fusion work motivates a
 private producer representation without contaminating extensional Sequence
 semantics; modern complete-fusion work motivates deterministic normalization of
 that producer calculus rather than reliance on accidental optimizer rewrites.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Control.Strict
import Data.Functor
import Data.Foldable
import Data.Bool
import Data.Semigroup
import Data.Sequence.Producer

module Sequence
  [Sequence Element Strategy uncons lower sequence-step sequence-transition
   ProductiveSequence FiniteSequence InfiniteSequence MaterializedSequence
   map filter prepend concat foldl foldr
   null? length reverse at nth head hd tail tl
   count empty? is-empty?
   take drop takeWhile dropWhile
   any? all? starts-with? ends-with?
   zip zipWith snoc]


class Evaluation (Strategy s) => Sequence s where
  type Element s
  type Strategy s
  uncons :: s -> (Element s, Strategy s s)?
  lower  :: s -> Producer (Strategy s) (Element s)

  lower values ->
    ProducerState sequence-transition values

  ;; Minimal complete definition: Element, Strategy, and uncons.
  ;;
  ;; Element s :: Type.
  ;; Strategy s :: Type -> Type.
  ;; `lower` is derived from uncons and may be overridden only by a coherent,
  ;; construction-total, demand-neutral, demand-refining producer representation.
  ;; The compiler must resolve Evaluation (Strategy s) when accepting the
  ;; Sequence instance so all generic consumers inherit that evidence once.


define sequence-step :: (a, e s)? -> Step e s a
  nil -> Stop
  (value, rest) -> Yield value rest


define sequence-transition :: Sequence s =>
  s -> Step (Strategy s) s (Element s)
  values -> sequence-step (uncons values)


instance Evaluation e => Sequence (Producer e a)
  type Element (Producer e a) = a
  type Strategy (Producer e a) = e

  uncons values -> observe-producer values
  lower values  -> values


class Sequence s => ProductiveSequence s where
  ;; Erased law certificate. The compiler must prove that every finite sequence
  ;; of uncons observations and demanded continuations terminates. No second
  ;; runtime observer is introduced.


class ProductiveSequence s => FiniteSequence s where
  ;; Erased well-foundedness certificate. Every continuation chain reaches nil
  ;; after finitely many visible observations. No runtime length/fuel is required.


class ProductiveSequence s => InfiniteSequence s where
  ;; Erased coinductive certificate. nil is unreachable and every finite visible
  ;; demand terminates.


class Foldable c => MaterializedSequence c where
  filter    :: (a -> Bool) -> c a -> c a
  prepend   :: a -> c a -> c a
  null?     :: c a -> Bool
  length    :: c a -> Int
  reverse   :: c a -> c a
  at        :: c a -> Int -> a?
  nth       :: c a -> Int -> a
  take      :: Int -> c a -> c a
  drop      :: Int -> c a -> c a
  takeWhile :: c a -> (a -> Bool) -> c a
  dropWhile :: c a -> (a -> Bool) -> c a
  any?      :: c a -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  all?      :: c a -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  zip       :: c a -> c b -> c (a, b)
  zipWith   :: c a -> c b -> (a -> b -> d) -> c d
  snoc      :: c a -> a -> c a

  law append-length :: Semigroup (c a) => c a -> c a -> Bool
    xs ys ->
      (length (append xs ys)) = ((length xs) + (length ys))

  law reverse-involution :: c a -> Bool
    xs -> (reverse (reverse xs)) = xs

  ;; Compatibility capability for the current finite Coll API. It is not part
  ;; of the mathematical definition of sequence or finiteness.


method starts-with? :: [a] -> [a] -> Bool
  values prefix -> __rt_starts_with? values prefix


method ends-with? :: [a] -> [a] -> Bool
  values suffix -> __rt_ends_with? values suffix


method concat :: Semigroup (c a) => c a -> c a -> c a
  xs ys -> append xs ys


method head :: [a] -> a
  :alias hd
  xs -> nth xs 0


method tail :: [a] -> [a]
  :alias tl
  xs -> drop 1 xs


method count :: [a] -> Int
  xs -> length xs


method empty? :: [a] -> Bool
  :alias is-empty?
  xs -> null? xs


method prepend :: a -> [a] -> [a]
  x xs -> __rt_prepend x xs


method null? :: [a] -> Bool
  []     -> True
  [_|_]  -> False


method length :: [a] -> Int
  []     -> 0
  [_|xs] -> 1 + (length xs)


method reverse-into :: [a] -> [a] -> [a]
  acc []     -> acc
  acc [x|xs] -> reverse-into (prepend x acc) xs


method reverse :: [a] -> [a]
  xs -> reverse-into [] xs


method at :: [a] -> Int -> a?
  _ n     | < 0 -> nil
  [] _          -> nil
  [x|_] 0       -> x
  [_|xs] n      -> at xs (n - 1)


method nth :: [a] -> Int -> a
  [] _ -> error "Sequence.nth: index out of bounds"
  [x|xs] n
    | n <= 0    -> x
    | otherwise -> nth xs (n - 1)


method take :: Int -> [a] -> [a]
  _ [] -> []
  n [x|xs]
      | n > 0     -> prepend x (take (n - 1) xs)
      | otherwise -> []


method drop :: Int -> [a] -> [a]
  _ [] -> []
  n [x|xs]
      | n > 0     -> drop (n - 1) xs
      | otherwise -> prepend x xs


method take-while :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> [a]
  [] _ -> []
  [x|xs] predicate
    | predicate x -> prepend x (take-while xs predicate)
    | otherwise   -> []


method drop-while :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> [a]
  [] _ -> []
  [x|xs] predicate
    | predicate x -> drop-while xs predicate
    | otherwise   -> prepend x xs


method filter :: (a -> Bool) -> [a] -> [a]
  _ [] -> []
  predicate [x|xs]
    | predicate x -> prepend x (filter predicate xs)
    | otherwise   -> filter predicate xs


method any? :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  [] _ -> False
  [x|xs] predicate -> if predicate x then True else any? xs predicate


method all? :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  [] _ -> True
  [x|xs] predicate
    | predicate x -> all? xs predicate
    | otherwise   -> False


method zip :: [a] -> [b] -> [(a, b)]
  [] _          -> []
  _ []          -> []
  [x|xs] [y|ys] -> prepend (x, y) (zip xs ys)


method zip-with :: [a] -> [b] -> (a -> b -> d) -> [d]
  [] _ _          -> []
  _ [] _          -> []
  [x|xs] [y|ys] f -> prepend (f x y) (zip-with xs ys f)


method snoc :: [a] -> a -> [a]
  [] value     -> list value
  [x|xs] value -> prepend x (snoc xs value)


instance Sequence [a]
  type Element [a] = a
  type Strategy [a] = Strict

  uncons [] -> nil
  uncons [value|rest] -> (value, strict rest)
  lower values -> ProducerState sequence-transition values


instance ProductiveSequence [a]


instance FiniteSequence [a]


instance MaterializedSequence Coll
  filter predicate xs -> filter predicate xs
  prepend value xs    -> __rt_prepend value xs
  null? xs            -> null? xs
  length xs           -> length xs
  reverse xs          -> reverse xs
  at xs index         -> at xs index
  nth xs index        -> nth xs index
  take amount xs      -> take amount xs
  drop amount xs      -> drop amount xs
  takeWhile xs p      -> take-while xs p
  dropWhile xs p      -> drop-while xs p
  any? xs p           -> any? xs p
  all? xs p           -> all? xs p
  zip xs ys           -> zip xs ys
  zipWith xs ys f     -> zip-with xs ys f
  snoc xs value       -> snoc xs value


tests
  :structure:
  assert-eq ([].null?)                            True        "null? empty"
  assert-eq ((list 1).null?)                      False       "null? nonempty"
  assert-eq ((list 1 2 3).length)                 3           "length"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2 3).reverse))         "(3 2 1)"   "reverse"
  assert-eq (show (concat (list 1 2) (list 3 4))) "(1 2 3 4)" "concat"
  assert-eq (show ((list 5 6 7).at 1))            "6"         "at present"
  assert-eq (show ((list 5 6 7).at 9))            "nil"       "at absent"
  assert-eq ((list 5 6 7).nth 2)                  7           "nth"
  assert-eq (head (list 5 6 7))                   5           "head"
  assert-eq (hd (list 5 6 7))                     5           "head alias"
  assert-eq (show (tail (list 5 6 7)))            "(6 7)"     "tail"
  assert-eq (show (tl (list 5 6 7)))              "(6 7)"     "tail alias"
  assert-eq (count (list 5 6 7))                  3           "count"
  assert-eq (empty? [])                            True        "empty?"
  assert-eq (is-empty? (list 5))                  False       "empty? alias"
  :structure:

  :take:
  assert-eq (show (take 2 (list 1 2 3))) "(1 2)" "take normal"
  assert-eq (show (take 0 (list 1 2)))   "()"    "take zero"
  assert-eq (show (take 5 (list 1 2)))   "(1 2)" "take beyond length"
  assert-eq (show (take 1 (list 42)))    "(42)"  "take singleton"
  :take:

  :drop:
  assert-eq (show (drop 2 (list 1 2 3 4))) "(3 4)" "drop normal"
  assert-eq (show (drop 0 (list 1 2)))     "(1 2)" "drop zero"
  assert-eq (show (drop 5 (list 1 2)))     "()"    "drop beyond length"
  assert-eq (show (drop 1 (list 42)))      "()"    "drop singleton"
  :drop:


  :map-runtime:
  assert-eq (show (map (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) (list 1 2 3))) "(2 3 4)" "map increment"
  assert-eq (show (map (lambda (x) (* x 2)) (list 0 1 2))) "(0 2 4)" "map double"
  assert-eq (show (map (lambda (x) x) (list 1 2))) "(1 2)" "map identity"
  assert-eq (show (map (lambda (_) 0) (list 1 2 3))) "(0 0 0)" "map constant"
  :map-runtime:

  :take-while:
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2 3 4).takeWhile (lambda (x) (< x 3)))) "(1 2)" "takeWhile less than 3"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).takeWhile (lambda (x) (< x 0))))     "()"    "takeWhile none"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).takeWhile (lambda (x) (< x 5))))     "(1 2)" "takeWhile all"
  :take-while:

  :drop-while:
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2 3 4).dropWhile (lambda (x) (< x 3)))) "(3 4)" "dropWhile less than 3"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).dropWhile (lambda (x) (< x 0))))     "(1 2)" "dropWhile none"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).dropWhile (lambda (x) (< x 5))))     "()"    "dropWhile all"
  :drop-while:

  :any:
  assert-eq ((list 1 2 3 4).any? (lambda (x) (< 2 x))) True  "any? finds element"
  assert-eq ((list 1 2 3).any? (lambda (x) (> x 9)))   False "any? missing"
  assert-eq ((list).any? (lambda (_) False))           False "any? empty list"
  :any:

  :all:
  assert-eq ((list 1 2 3).all? (lambda (x) (< x 5))) True  "all? all satisfy"
  assert-eq ((list 1 2 3).all? (lambda (x) (< x 3))) False "all? one fails"
  assert-eq ((list).all? (lambda (_) False))         True  "all? empty list"
  :all:

  :zip:
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).zip (list 3 4))) "((1 3) (2 4))" "zip pair"
  assert-eq (show ((list 1).zip (list 2 3)))   "((1 2))"       "zip uneven"
  :zip:

  :zipWith:
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).zipWith (list 3 4) (lambda (x y) (+ x y)))) "(4 6)" "zipWith sum"
  assert-eq (show ((list 2 3).zipWith (list 4) (lambda (x y) (* x y))))   "(8)"   "zipWith uneven"
  :zipWith:

  :snoc:
  assert-eq (show ((list 1 2).snoc 3)) "(1 2 3)" "snoc nonempty"
  assert-eq (show ([].snoc 3))         "(3)"     "snoc empty"
  :snoc:

  :filter:
  assert-eq (show (filter (lambda (x) (< x 3)) (list 1 2 3 4))) "(1 2)" "filter less"
  assert-eq (show (filter (lambda (x) (> x 5)) (list 1 2))) "()" "filter none"
  :filter:

  :concat:
  assert-eq (show (concat (list 1 2) (list 3 4))) "(1 2 3 4)" "Sequence concat lists"
  assert-eq (show (concat "ab" "cd"))             "abcd"      "Sequence concat String"
  :concat:
